// The user interface: the components most applications are built from, and
// the vocabulary they share.
//
// What is here is the portable core -- a `button` means the same thing on
// the web, on a phone, in a desktop toolkit and in a terminal, and a
// platform that renders it differently still renders the same declaration.
// Specialised surfaces live in packages under this one, so that a program
// pays for a drawing canvas or a document viewer only by importing it:
//
//	sngl:ui/draw    a canvas and the 2D shapes it hosts
//
// The components fall into five groups:
//
//	display    show a value and do not edit it
//	form       controls the user edits, each with a two-way bound value prop
//	layout     containers, which place their children
//	nav        move the user somewhere else
//	overlay    paint above the rest of the tree
//
// `node` is the family all five belong to, and `window` is the surface they
// are placed on -- the one member of `root`, the family a package body
// accepts. The two are here rather than in an application-shell package of
// their own because a window is where widgets go: what it hosts is `node`,
// so any package holding it would import this one and be imported back.
//
// `Style`, the style enums and the event payloads sit here rather than in a
// package of their own because every component in every one of those
// packages refers to them; they are the vocabulary, not a separate concern.
//
// Nothing here is in scope until it is imported. `import <alias> "sngl:ui"`
// qualifies its declarations (`alias.vbox`), and is also how a platform
// overrides a component (`component <alias>.vbox`); it is the form this
// repository writes. `import . "sngl:ui"` writes them unqualified and remains
// supported.
